From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lukasz Sobala <lsobala1@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 13:16:09 +0200
Subject: riscv: dts: sophgo: note that Milk-V Duo S uart2 pads are Bluetooth

uart2 is enabled but unusable, and worse than unusable if someone routes it:
cvi_board_init() in the vendor bootloader points the UART2_RX/TX/CTS/RTS pads at
uart4, which is where this board keeps its Bluetooth controller. Nothing in the
DTS said so, so the trap was invisible.

Comment only, no functional change. The node is deliberately left enabled -
those pads reach the pin header, so a board that wires uart2 to something else
can route it - but anyone doing that now knows what it costs.

Signed-off-by: Armbian <info@armbian.com>
---
 arch/riscv/boot/dts/sophgo/sg2000-milkv-duo-s.dts | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/riscv/boot/dts/sophgo/sg2000-milkv-duo-s.dts b/arch/riscv/boot/dts/sophgo/sg2000-milkv-duo-s.dts
index 111111111111..222222222222 100644
--- a/arch/riscv/boot/dts/sophgo/sg2000-milkv-duo-s.dts
+++ b/arch/riscv/boot/dts/sophgo/sg2000-milkv-duo-s.dts
@@ -254,6 +254,14 @@ &uart1 {
 	status = "okay";
 };
 
+/*
+ * Enabled, but its pads are not routed here and must not be: cvi_board_init() in
+ * the vendor bootloader points UART2_RX/TX/CTS/RTS at uart4, which is this
+ * board's Bluetooth controller. Routing them back to uart2 takes Bluetooth with
+ * it. The node stays because those pads reach the pin header, so a board that
+ * wires uart2 elsewhere can still route it; the uart2 overlay does exactly
+ * that, on two pads that have nothing to do with Bluetooth.
+ */
 &uart2 {
 	status = "okay";
 };
